**Both domain collisions I found were with domains I already owned. Search your own registrar for the brand name before you build on a new TLD.** One aged domain and two collisions, reported as they happened, not a statistically powered study.

## What happened when an aged domain got repurposed? {% #aged-domain-case %}

bestreviews.co.uk was bought aged for its water-softener and home-products backlink profile. It launched in April 2026 as a cost-comparison hub with 95 towns times 10 services of programmatic pages, then was rebuilt reviews-first in late July: on 26 July, 1,130 of those town/service pages were deleted as thin spun content and wildcard-301'd into 10 national hubs.

Search Console recorded 4 clicks and 17,069 impressions from 20 June to 15 September 2026. Average position across the period was 82.7.

| Metric | Value, 20 June to 15 September 2026 |
|---|---|
| Clicks | 4 |
| Impressions | 17,069 |
| Average CTR | 0.02% |
| Average position | 82.7 |

Daily impressions ran 150-650 through June and July, then fell to under 40 a day from 21 August. Average position moved from the mid-80s to the 60s and 70s over the same window, mostly sitting 59-75. That shift followed the 26 July deletion of 1,130 town pages, so it reflects fewer low-ranked URLs pulling the average down, not pages climbing. Nothing reached page one, before or after.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="4" label="clicks from 17,069 impressions on bestreviews.co.uk, 20 June to 15 September 2026" source="Search Console" /%}
{% stat value="1,130" label="thin town/service pages deleted from bestreviews.co.uk on 26 July 2026" source="Author's own portfolio data" /%}
{% /stats %}

Domain age and inherited backlinks did not rescue spun location pages. One case, but it is the case the aged-domain pitch says should not happen: real backlink history, still stuck at position 80+ on thin content, and the only ranking-shape change came from deleting pages, not from the domain's age.

{% pullquote cite="From this post's aged-domain case" %}
real backlink history, still stuck at position 80+ on thin content, and the only ranking-shape change came from deleting pages, not from the domain's age.
{% /pullquote %}

## What is a domain collision? {% #domain-collision-definition %}

A domain collision is a second live domain carrying the same or a similar brand name as an unlaunched new build. A portfolio audit on 29 July 2026 found two domains matching this pattern and a third that turned out to be something else.

| Live domain | Intended new build | Condition found |
|---|---|---|
| dampfree.co.uk | dampfree.uk | Dormant WordPress install, one 2019 post, homepage retitled with no real content |
| bestwalkingpads.com | bestwalkingpads.co.uk | Live branded site with broken navigation on every main category link |

A third domain checked in the same audit, ideeeas.com, turned out to be a misconfiguration rather than a collision: its own homepage was pointed at the wrong WordPress page and a Rank Math redirect rule sent every unmatched path to the sitemap.

dampfree.co.uk carried a single "Hello World" post from 2019 under an old web-design-agency template. Its homepage had been retitled "DampFree UK – Moisture Absorbers" with none of the actual DampFree content behind it. bestwalkingpads.com carried real "Best Walking Pads" branding, but its navigation linked to /reviews, /category/guides and /category/reviews, and all three returned 404, with no posts under /blog.

As of 18 September 2026: dampfree.co.uk is still live in the same broken state, and its intended replacement dampfree.uk has not launched. bestwalkingpads.com no longer resolves at all, and bestwalkingpads.co.uk has not launched either.

## What should you check before launching a new domain? {% #pre-launch-checklist %}

Search the exact brand name across every TLD you already hold before building on a new one. Both collisions were on domains I already owned, not domains a competitor held. Checking the target domain alone misses this.

Once a collision is found, the fix is a decision:

- Redirect the old domain to the new one once the new build is live.
- Repurpose the old domain for unrelated content if the brand should not carry across both.
- Retire the old domain if neither option earns its renewal cost.

Neither case above was fixed at the time; both needed WordPress admin or DNS access, not a code change. Status on 18 September 2026: dampfree.co.uk unresolved, bestwalkingpads.com gone, neither new build launched.

## Does buying an aged domain still make sense? {% #aged-domain-verdict %}

Buying an aged domain buys its backlinks. The ranking still has to be earned by the content you put on it. bestreviews.co.uk kept a backlink history built for one niche and moved into a different one; the domain's age did not follow it there.

{% pullquote cite="From this post's verdict on aged domains" %}
Buying an aged domain buys its backlinks. The ranking still has to be earned by the content you put on it.
{% /pullquote %}

Treat an aged domain as link equity for a closely related niche. Check its link profile and history against the planned content before paying for age.

## How was this checked? {% #methodology %}

This covers one aged domain and two verified collisions from my own portfolio, not a statistically powered study. Domain-collision findings are dated 29 July 2026; current live/resolving status is dated 18 September 2026. Search Console figures for bestreviews.co.uk are dated 18 September 2026, for the window 20 June to 15 September 2026.

**Evidence limits:** these are properties I own; nothing here was independently audited by a third party. Domain status can change after publication, check current status directly before treating either collision case as fixed or unfixed.

